[2026-10-16T20:11:26.575Z] [INFO]             "text": "We have created the file with comments. Now we need to ensure it follows Scala best practices and idioms.\n The code looks good: an object with a main method, using println.\n\n Let's now create the ci directory and the workflow file.\n\n We'll create the workflow as per instructions: ci/test-hello-world.yml\n\n The workflow should:\n   - Trigger on push to main and pull requests\n   - Set up Scala (we can use Scala SDK setup from GitHub Actions, e.g., using coursier or setup-scala action)\n   - Run the program and verify the output.\n\n Since we are in a test repository, we can use a simple setup.\n\n Let's create the workflow.\n\n We'll use the following steps:\n   - Checkout the code\n   - Setup Scala (we can use the setup-scala action from GitHub Marketplace, but note we cannot rely on external actions without internet? Actually we can, but let's use a simple method: install JDK and Scala via apt-get or use coursier.\n\n However, the issue says: \"Set up the appropriate Scala runtime/compiler\"\n\n We can use the following:\n\n   - Install JDK (if not present) and then install Scala via coursier or download.\n\n But to keep it simple and since we are in a Linux environment, we can use:\n\n   - Install OpenJDK and then download Scala from the official site.\n\n Alternatively, we can use the setup-scala action. Since we are allowed to use external actions, let's use:\n\n   - actions/setup-java for JDK\n   - Then use coursier to install Scala.\n\n However, note that the workflow must be self-contained and work in the test environment.\n\n Let's look at the example in the issue: they didn't specify a particular action.\n\n We'll create a workflow that:\n\n   - Uses ubuntu-latest\n   - Sets up JDK (using actions/setup-java)\n   - Then installs Scala via coursier (or we can just use the scala package if available?)\n\n But to avoid relying on external actions that might not be available, we can do:\n\n   - Install